In France, voting is done in the traditional way: voters individually go to the ballot box, place the pre-printed ballot paper, which has already been marked with their chosen candidate, into the postal box.
After the voter's identity has been verified by the officials, the voter will place the ballot envelope into the ballot box and sign the ballot submission form.
The electoral law of France is relatively strict in defining the steps for enforcement of law.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent to allow voters and observers to witness that no envelopes were present in the box at the beginning of the voting process, and that no additional envelopes were added other than those from voters who were counted and accepted.
Candidates may send representatives to serve as witnesses at every stage of the process. In the evening, votes will be counted by volunteers under strict supervision by the respective stage coordinators.
