Taiwan has its origins traced back to the 15th century, a period when European navigators sailed through the region and recorded the island's name as Ilha Formosa, meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the East India Company established a base in the southwestern part of Taiwan and began shifting its focus from traditional rice production to cultivating rice and sugar, employing Chinese laborers to work on its plantations.
In 1683, the Qing imperial forces (1644–1912) captured the western and northern regions of Taiwan and in 1885 declared Taiwan a province of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, following Japan's victory in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Shimonoseki Treaty, by which it ceded sovereignty over Taiwan to Japan. Japan then ruled the island until 1945.
