School visits are an important part of every class. Teachers often take students to various places, and buses are not always an option.
Technology offers solutions by simulating real-world travel experiences. Students can view various physical inventions from the museum, visit the aquarium, or enjoy beautiful art—all while sitting in their classroom.
Sharing real-life educational experiences is a great way to illustrate journeys and share insights with future classes.
For example, each year students from Bennet School in North Carolina design a website about their annual trip to State Capital. The website is updated each year, but the old versions are still kept as a photo album.
Blogs can also help improve students' writing. While students often begin their blog experiences with a sense of freedom and unusual creativity, the perspective of the general public will eventually change that.
Since students are the most important target audience, bloggers have begun trying to improve their writing to avoid criticism.
Blog writers "have also encouraged students to gain greater knowledge and understanding of the world around them." The need to meet readers' interests has motivated students to be more thoughtful and attentive (Toto, 2004).
Writing blogs is a tool that motivates collaboration and encourages students to extend their learning beyond regular classroom lessons.
Proper use of blogs "can help students become analysts and critics to a greater extent; by actively responding to online tools, students can establish their own positions in other people's writings, while constructing their own perspectives within a specific context" (Oravec, 2002).
