Goma is a tourist town of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, located in the eastern part near the country of Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava from the Nyiragongo volcano, which buried most of the streets in the city, particularly those in the city center.
Although Goma is relatively safe, visits to areas outside Goma should be thoroughly investigated to understand the ongoing conflict situations remaining in North Kivu province.
The town is also a destination for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along with the cheapest gorilla trekking route in Africa.
You can use a boda-boda (motorcycle taxi) to go around Goma. The standard price (locally) is approximately 500 Congolese francs for a short ride.
Together with its connotation of inaccessibility, the term "Timbuktu" has been used as a metaphor for a remote, faraway place.
