The Sundarbans is the largest mangrove forest in the world, stretching up to 80 kilometers (50 miles) inland into the borders of Bangladesh and the remote coastal regions of India.
The Sundarbans area has been designated and recognized as a World Heritage Site by UNESCO. A portion of the forest within the Indian territory is known as the Sundarbans National Park.
Forests are not merely water sources—they include some of the last remaining parts of this vast forest that once extended all the way to the Gangetic plains.
The Sundarbans region covers an area of 3,850 km², about one-third of which is covered by water/mangrove areas.
The Sundarbans National Park has been a protected wildlife sanctuary since 1966, and it is estimated that there are currently around 400 Royal Bengal tigers and approximately 30,000 deer in the area.
